course: world
lesson: 46
title: Countries, peoples and languages of the world
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: About 8 billion people live on Earth, in about 195 countries; a country has its own borders, its own government and its own symbols, such as a flag and an anthem.
explain: Countries are very different in size: Russia is the largest country by area and Canada the second, while Vatican City, inside the city of Rome, is so small that you can walk across it in a quarter of an hour.
explain: People speak about 7,000 different languages; some are spoken by hundreds of millions, like Chinese, English, Hindi and Spanish, and some only by a few hundred people in one valley.
explain: English is the main language of the United States, the United Kingdom, Ireland, Australia, New Zealand and most of Canada, because people from Britain settled there long ago; but in Quebec in Canada most people speak French, Irish is spoken in Ireland, Welsh in Wales, Maori in New Zealand, and many Native American and Aboriginal languages are much older than English in those lands.
explain: Languages are related like families: English, German and Dutch are cousins in the Germanic family, so many words look alike, such as water and Wasser; Ukrainian, Polish and Czech are cousins in the Slavic family; Spanish, French and Italian all grew from Latin.
fact: About 8 billion people live on Earth.
fact: There are about 195 countries in the world.
fact: Russia is the largest country by area, and Canada is the second largest.
fact: Vatican City is the smallest country in the world.
fact: India and China are the two countries with the most people, each with more than 1.4 billion.
fact: People in the world speak about 7,000 different languages.
fact: Mandarin Chinese has the most native speakers of any language.
fact: English is the language most widely learned as a second language.
fact: Canada has two official languages, English and French.
fact: English, German and Dutch belong to the Germanic family of languages.
fact: Spanish, French, Italian and Portuguese grew from Latin and are called Romance languages.
fact: Ukraine is a large country in eastern Europe, its capital is Kyiv and its language, Ukrainian, is a Slavic language.
exercise: About how many people live on Earth? | About 8 billion | About 8 million | About 800 thousand | About 80 billion
exercise: About how many countries are there in the world? | About 195 | About 20 | About 1,000 | About 7,000
exercise: Which is the largest country in the world by area? | Russia | China | Canada | The United States
exercise: Which is the smallest country in the world? | Vatican City | Liechtenstein | Monaco | Luxembourg
exercise: Which two countries have the most people? | India and China | Russia and Canada | The United States and Mexico | Brazil and Australia
exercise: About how many languages are spoken in the world? | About 7,000 | About 195 | About 50 | About 700,000
exercise: Which language has the most native speakers? | Mandarin Chinese | English | Spanish | Hindi
exercise: Which language is most widely learned as a second language? | English | Mandarin Chinese | Latin | French
exercise: Which two languages are official in Canada? | English and French | English and Spanish | French and German | English and Inuit only
exercise: Why is English spoken in Australia, New Zealand and the United States? | British settlers brought it | English was first spoken in Australia | It is the oldest language of those lands | Every country must speak English by law
exercise: To which family of languages does English belong? | The Germanic family | The Slavic family, like Polish | The Romance family, like French | The Chinese family of languages
exercise: Which languages grew from Latin? | Spanish, French and Italian | English, German and Dutch | Ukrainian, Polish and Czech | Chinese, Japanese and Korean
exercise: What is the capital of Ukraine? | Kyiv | Warsaw | Moscow | Prague
exercise: Which language is spoken by the Maori people of New Zealand? | Maori | Welsh | Irish | French
exercise: What does every country have? | Its own borders, government and flag | Its own continent and ocean | Its own language that no one else speaks | Its own mountain and river
test: A girl in Montreal, Quebec, chats with her neighbours in a language that is not English. Which language is it most likely? | French | Spanish | German | Maori
test: Which is true about languages? | There are about 7,000, and some are spoken by very few people | There are only about 195, one for each country | Every country has exactly one language | All languages come from English
test: A small country lies completely inside the city of Rome. Which is it? | Vatican City | Monaco | San Marino | Luxembourg
test: A boy in London notices that the German word Haus looks like the English house. Why? | English and German are related Germanic languages | German is a dialect of English | English took all its words from German last century | All languages in the world have the same words
test: Were there languages in North America and Australia before English arrived? | Yes, many native languages | No, people there had no languages at all | No, those lands were empty until English came | Only one language was spoken in each land
test: Which country covers the most land on Earth? | Russia | Canada | China | The United States
test: If you met people from ten different countries, which language would most of them most likely have learned at school as a second language? | English | Mandarin Chinese | Welsh | Latin
test: Which statement about countries with the most people is true? | India and China each have over a billion | Russia has more people than India and China together | Australia has many more people than India | Canada has more people than China and Japan
test: Which pair of languages are close relatives? | Spanish and Italian | English and Japanese | French and Chinese | Ukrainian and Arabic
test: A pen pal writes from Kyiv in a Slavic language. Where does she live? | In Ukraine, in eastern Europe | In Ireland, in western Europe | In Canada, in North America | In Australia, near the coast
test: smallest country in the whole world which | Vatican City | Monaco | Ireland | New Zealand
test: why's spanish n italian sound kinda the same | Both grew from Latin, so they are Romance languages | Both grew from German | Spain and Italy have always shared one single language | They borrowed words from English
test: my brother says English has the most native speakers. true? | No, Mandarin Chinese has the most native speakers | Yes, English has the most native speakers | Yes, more than any other two together | No, Spanish has the most of all
test: Olivia reckons Canada only speaks English. right? | No, Canada has two official languages, English and French | Yes, English is the only one | Yes, French is banned there | No, Canada's language is Spanish, like in Mexico next door
exam: Identify the largest country of the world by area. | Russia | Canada | China | The United States
exam: Identify the smallest country of the world. | Vatican City | Monaco | Luxembourg | Malta
exam: Identify the language possessing the greatest number of native speakers. | Mandarin Chinese | English | Spanish | Hindi
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the Romance languages. | They derive from Latin, as Spanish, French and Italian | They derive from Greek, as Spanish and German | They include English, German and Dutch | They derive from the Slavic languages
